{
  "raw": {
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Facilities-Specialist---Housing-and-Residence-Life_R105068": {
      "title": "Facilities Specialist - Housing and Residence Life",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nPosition Summary: Responsible for the day-to-day housing facilities related assessment, emergencies, preventative drills, inspections, inventory management, and follow-up. Salary & Benefits:  Minimum starting salary of $48,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits packag e including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.  Primary Duties: Support institution/division/department mission, vision, and values. Assess and maintain inventory and provide repair and replacement recommendations for items such as common area furniture/equipment, room/apartment furniture, and staff furniture. Establish contacts and relationships with vendors for selection, ordering, installation, replacements, repairs, donations, and other maintenance of FFE in the housing system Work with the Assignments Team to add and reduce sets of furniture based on occupancy needs. Coordinate semesterly building walk-throughs and room/apartment inspections with Residence Life staff. Perform regular building inspections and maintain accurate records and reports. Report policy/procedure violations to appropriate staff and recommend edits to the HRL Community Living Standards to supervisor as appropriate. Assist with damage and fee spreadsheet, updating and refining the list on a yearly basis Coordinate and implement Health and Safety checks during Fall and Winter semesters. Run point with GVPD, Allendale Fire, and Res. Life for fire drills on the Valley campus. Assist with sustainability initiatives within housing for recycling and energy conservation. Liaison to Facilities Services for floods, fire damage, mold, pest concerns, preventative maintenance, etc., connecting with residents, staff and contractors as appropriate. Oversee the Winter Hall fitness center equipment; scheduling repairs, maintaining condition, and ordering replacement parts and machines as needed. Assist with the opening and closing procedures relative to facility use, to include training on the completion of Housing Condition Summaries and auditing/assessment of building/unit damages. Assist with IT inventory, tracking and removal. Assist with Camps and Conference operations as able, utilizing student staff for furniture moves/adjustments, providing bins, hanging shower curtains, etc. Hire, train, supervise, and evaluate assigned staff, fostering a culture of mentorship and professional growth. Instruct employees in proper receiving and delivery procedures as well as safety measures. Performs other duties as required/assigned by manager. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Bachelor’s degree or a minimum of one year experience of related work experience and/or relevant training. Experience training, mentoring, or supervising team members. Minimum of one year experience of related work experience and/or relevant training. Demonstrated experience to successfully maintain positive intercultural and interpersonal relationships. Demonstrated experience to successfully work under pressure and meet deadlines. Demonstrated experience in creative problem solving. Demonstrated experience to successfully provide quality customer service. Demonstrated experience to successfully maintain satisfactory work performance. Demonstrated experience in organization and attention to detail. Demonstrated experience with a variety of computer software applications (examples: SQBX, THD, Microsoft and Google programs). Demonstrated experience to successfully work independently, organize own work and coordinate work activities of others. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   3+ years related work experience. Experience working within a college or university housing setting. Experience with GOFMX, Workday, and The Housing Director systems or equivalent. Experience in facilities emergency response and resolution, including customer service and vendor coordination. Experience with vendor relationship management. How to Apply:  Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Adam Tate at tateam@gvsu.edu. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.  Application Deadline:   Application review begins October 15, 2026. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Centralized-Client-Onboarding-Specialist---MI-SBDC--part-time-temp-hourly-_R105141": {
      "title": "Centralized Client Onboarding Specialist - MI-SBDC (part-time temp hourly)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nAs a temporary employee of GVSU, the Centralized Client Onboarding Specialist serves as the critical \"front door\" for the Michigan SBDC, welcoming and onboarding new clients into our organization. Under the direction of the Centralized Client Intake Manager, this role is responsible for executing a high-performance, standardized statewide onboarding process, keeping the client at the center of what we do. Specialists will use standardized scripts, triage logic, scheduling, CRM, and AI systems to ensure every client is efficiently assessed and matched with the right consultant based on region and expertise. Employment is expected to be a 1-year term, and employment renewal is contingent upon available funding. Part-time position working a maximum of 29 hours per week. Primary Duties: 1. Call Management & Client Triage Intake Execution: Provide a professional, consistent, and welcoming first point of contact for all clients. Welcome Meetings: Prepare for and conduct scheduled phone calls with potential new clients. Introduce Michigan SBDC services, gather accurate information to confirm eligibility for no cost consulting, and route clients to the appropriate consultant. Inbound Call Routing: Manage and triage inbound calls to the primary client-facing phone number. Complex Case Escalation: Escalate complex client inquiries or system discrepancies to the Centralized Client Intake Manager for resolution. Consultant Success: Conduct all onboarding activities to set the stage for a high-quality initial client-consultant meeting. 2. Systems Management & AI Utilization System Compliance: Accurately record client data in Salesforce and use Slack for internal team communication. AI Utilization: Use Intake-specific approved AI tools to ensure high-fidelity, consultant-ready data extraction from onboarding sessions. Calendar Management: Utilize Google Workspace and Calendly for all onboarding sessions to prevent double-bookings. 3. Operational Adherence & Data Tracking Protocol Adherence: Strictly follow all standardized scripts, knowledge bases, and standard operating procedures (SOPs) developed by the Lead Center. Session Tracking: Accurately log data and information for each onboarding session for use in consulting and reporting.  Workflow Participation: Participate as required in quality reviews of Salesforce data entry and onboarding processes.    Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities : Communication: Exceptional verbal and written communication skills, with a strong telephone presence.  Customer Success Experience: Experience in direct customer services, preferably high-volume customer-facing scheduling and service, where maintaining a high standard of professional \"first impression\" is critical. Process Orientation: Proven ability to follow Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) and standardized scripts to ensure consistency. Technical Dexterity: Demonstrated ability to swiftly navigate and adapt to multiple digital environments, including the rapid learning and integration of new productivity tools (e.g., Google Workspace, Gemini, Calendly) and CRM platforms (e.g., Salesforce) into daily workflows. Systemic Thinking (Triage Mindset): The ability to distinguish between administrative/triage needs, including escalations, and substantive consulting or diagnosis.   Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities : Technical Proficiency: Proficiency with digital productivity tools in the Michigan SBDC tech stack, including Google Workspace, Calendly, Slack and Salesforce.  Spanish Proficiency: Conversational and business Spanish is a plus.   Work Location & Schedule Employment Status: This is a temporary hourly position employed through GVSU for a 1-year term. Employment renewal is contingent upon available funding. Flexible Work Arrangement (FWA): This position is a remote role. Candidates must currently live in Michigan or be willing to relocate prior to the start date. Relocation assistance is not provided. Shift Options (Capacity Management): Shifts will be scheduled to ensure 100% coverage during business hours.  About the Department/Division For details about Seidman College of Business, visit www.gvsu.edu/seidman . For details about the Unit, visit https://michigansbdc.org/ Salary : Hourly rate $16.50 (maximum 29 hours per week) How to Apply:  Apply online at GVSU Careers to be considered; applicants must upload the following (in PDF format) to the online application ( five documents or five MB maximum ): Cover letter Curriculum vitae Unofficial copy of transcript   If selected for an interview, you will be asked to provide the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three professional references. You will also be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. If you have questions or need assistance, call Human Resources at 616-331-2215. Questions about this position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Sienna Mavima, mavimasi@gvsu.edu .   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future   Application Deadline: This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Place-based-Education-Specialist---College-of-Education-and-Community-Innovation_R105124": {
      "title": "Place-based Education Specialist - College of Education and Community Innovation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nThis position works with the Center for Educational Partnerships (CEP), Groundswell Stewardship Initiative (GSI) advisory teams, and a network of community partners to support PK-12 schools in the adoption and active use of place-based stewardship education (PBSE) principles and related practices within their institutions. Primary Duties : Implement existing grant and program requirements, including project management, budgeting, and reporting Collaborate with CEP staff and other GSI stakeholders to develop new grant/funding proposals Work with CEP staff and other GSI stakeholders to develop, market, implement, and evaluate high-impact professional learning opportunities around PBSE Support PK-12 educators in the field to facilitate the development and implementation of student-led PBSE projects Support PK-12 teachers and community partners in the development of mutually beneficial relationships that support student/community stewardship Work with CEP staff and other stakeholders to identify and implement strategies for program development, sustainability, and continuous improvement Perform other duties as assigned per grant agreement and/or leadership Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Required : Bachelor's degree in related field A minimum of two (2) years of experience in K-12 focused classroom/programing, with a focus on developing learning experiences that align with K-12 curriculum standards A minimum of two (2) years of experience planning, facilitating, and evaluating high-impact professional development for adult learners Familiarity with and commitment to PBSE principles and practices of PBSE principles and practices Strong skills in written and verbal communication, organization, and conceptualization Ability to design and deliver effective presentations to a variety of audiences Strong skills in cultural competence and collaboration, including an ability to work effectively with multiple stakeholders and within large and small teams on various projects Demonstrated commitment to community-based environmental stewardship with a focus on diversity, equity, inclusion, and racial justice Basic skills in fund development, including a basic knowledge of writing effective proposals, managing and reporting on funded projects, and cultivating professional relationships with funders Willingness to learn new approaches to teaching and learning in order to support university and K-12 educators and students in the planning and practice of place-based stewardship education Proficiency with office technology and software Preferred Qualifications and Education : Master's degree in educational leadership, nonprofit management, or related field Five (5) years of experience in K-12 focused classroom/programing, with a focus on developing learning experiences that align with K-12 curriculum standards Experience working in higher education Experience with project management software Experience developing, managing, and interpreting/applying project and program evaluation for organizational reporting and continuous improvement Working Conditions: Normal office environment. Some travel may be required. Salary: Minimum starting salary range of $50,000-$52,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.  How To Apply: Attach your cover letter, resume, and three professional references, with contact information. Applications missing required documents will not be considered . The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. If you have questions about the position, contact search chair Clay Pelon at pelonc@gvsu.edu .   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.     Application Deadline: October 25, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Academic-Department-Coordinator---Electrical-and-Computering-Engineering-Department_R105099": {
      "title": "Academic Department Coordinator - Electrical and Computering Engineering Department",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nThe Academic Department Coordinator supports the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering through various responsibilities and functions in which discretion and judgment concerning the procedures to use or policies to follow is required. Salary & Benefits: C3 Level. Minimum starting wage of $21.10 per hour. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.  Essential Functions: Maintain and analyze budgetary records for the department by performing bookkeeping related duties such as reconciling, monitoring, making, and tracking expenditures, preparing budget statements regarding the status of accounts, making recommendations as to projected budgets. Create requisitions for large purchases, ensuring that appropriate approvals have been obtained and that all necessary documentation is present. Coordinate personnel reviews and actions, including scheduling unit meetings, creating documents, taking minutes, assembling portfolios, and ensuring timely delivery of meeting materials. Coordinate and monitor course evaluation process. Support college event planning, implementation, and communication for multi-day large-scale events. Disseminate information that may require explanation and interpretation of established university and departmental policies and procedures, and direct individuals to sources of additional information. Maintain digital and physical filing system according to retention/disposal policies and best practices. Serve as administrative assistant for department staff searches, including scheduling interviews, coordinating travel, organizing preparation of office space, and processing hiring forms. Generate reports, memorandums, correspondence, presentation materials, brochures, spreadsheets and graphics, and other similar materials using various computer systems. Perform and coordinate scheduling tasks and support services associated with preparing and making arrangements for meetings, conferences, exhibits, travel, or other related duties for both internal and external contacts. Receive information in various forms and determine the appropriate method of processing. Responsible for the coordination/completion and the quality of the clerical work for the department/office which includes making and receiving calls, providing information, or routing calls to appropriate person; typing memos and other correspondence; maintaining office filing system. Hire, train, and supervise student employees. Act as liaison between department chair, faculty, staff, students, and outside organizations. Track and support faculty travel arrangements Assist faculty with ordering textbooks. Maintain supervisor’s calendar and multiple calendars. Order and track inventory of office supplies. Other related duties as assigned. Non-Essential Functions: Assist in academic year scheduling of courses, classrooms, times, and faculty for the department. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: High school graduate or equivalent. Minimum of three years of relevant work experience. Experience with various computer software applications such as Microsoft Office and/or equivalent. Demonstrated experience to successfully operate office equipment. Data entry experience. Typing and spelling competency with grammar and proofreading skills. Possess strong verbal and written communication skills. Demonstrated experience to successfully provide quality customer service Demonstrated experience to successfully work independently, organize own work and coordinate work activities of others. Ability to work under pressure and meet deadlines. Demonstrated experience to successfully maintain satisfactory work performance and attendance records. Ability to successfully maintain positive intercultural and interpersonal relationships. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Some college course work. Higher education experience. Supervisory experience. Familiarity with graphic design and photo editing software. Previous experience managing social media platforms and websites. Travel planning and booking experience. Event planning experience. How to Apply: Attach your cover letter and resume. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Heidi Jiao, jiaoh@gvsu.edu.  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.  Application Deadline: Application closes October 20, 2026 at 11:59pm. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/WGVU-Development-Officer--Foundation-Relations_R105130": {
      "title": "WGVU Development Officer, Foundation Relations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nThis position oversees grant funding opportunities that support WGVU goals and priorities in the areas of technology, education, IDEA, programming, community impact, and other areas as they arise. This role serves as a liaison between WGVU, the GVSU Office of Sponsored Programs, University Institutional Advancement, and GVSU Grants Accounting. Salary & Benefits: Salary range of $57,500 - $67,000, commensurate with experience Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties Responsible for the grants administration process, including researching potential funders, evaluation of RFPs, overseeing management of funded projects, as well as tracking and reporting requirements. Write, review, and edit proposals to foundations and other potential funders. Identify, manage, and secure philanthropic gifts from private, corporate, and public foundations. Develop and maintain relationships with local, state, and federal funding agencies. Provide leadership for identifying, qualifying, cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding major foundation grant makers to increase gifts to WGVU. Work to establish long-term partnerships and strengthen existing relationships with foundation sector and community organizations. Serve as a liaison between WGVU, the GVSU Office of Sponsored Programs, University Institutional Advancement, and GVSU Grants Accounting. Manage a portfolio of foundation donors and prospects. Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Bachelor's degree in related field. Minimum of three to five (3-5) years of successful grant writing experience in nonprofit, public broadcasting, or educational setting. Proven experience in researching, writing, and administering grants. Ability to take initiative, collaborate, and use innovative techniques to prepare grant applications. Ability to design, develop, and implement funded projects. Ability to monitor budgets and expenditures to adhere to grant agreements. Ability to contribute to a work environment in which integrity, collaboration, respect, and a strong work ethic are valued. Experience with various computer software applications (Microsoft Office, Outlook, Teams, Monday.com, Raiser’s Edge/CRM, etc). Commitment to WGVU’s vision of diversity, inclusion, and equity. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously and meet deadlines. Ability to work within a collaborative multiplatform culture and environment. Ability to demonstrate professionalism, punctuality, and work self-sufficiently with little direction when required. Ability to maintain a flexible work schedule, including some nights, weekends, and holidays. Valid driver’s license and ability to work on location as needed. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Master’s degree. How to Apply : Attach your cover letter, resume and writing sample. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Terri Kenney at kenneyt@gvsu.edu. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future. Application Deadlin e: Application review begins on October 26, 2026 and this posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Clinical-Services-Manager--RN----Campus-Health-Center_R105108": {
      "title": "R105108 Clinical Operations Manager (RN) – Campus Health Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nThis position serves as the primary operational leader for daily clinic activities and is responsible for coordinating patient care delivery, staff workflow, and clinic operations within the Campus Health Center. Salary and Benefits: $85,500 to $102,800, commensurate with experience Comprehensive benefits package  including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.    Primary Duties: Lead and coordinate daily clinical operations to support safe, timely, efficient, and student-centered care. Coordinate patient flow, clinical staffing, room assignments, and daily workflows in collaboration with nurse practitioners, medical assistants, office staff, and the medical director. Supervise assigned clinical support staff, including hiring, onboarding, scheduling, work delegation, performance evaluation, coaching, competency assessment, and professional development. Provide nursing assessment, triage, patient education, medication administration, care coordination, and other direct nursing services as needed and within the registered nurse’s scope of practice. Address operational and patient-care concerns in real time and facilitate appropriate escalation to the director, medical director, or another level of care. Collaborate with the medical director and healthcare providers to implement and maintain clinical policies, standing orders, protocols, and evidence-based practices. This position does not independently supervise the clinical practice of advanced practice providers. Oversee systems for diagnostic testing, laboratory results, referrals, medication follow-up, and other clinical activities requiring timely review and completion. Lead infection-prevention activities, including staff education, exposure-response procedures, communicable-disease precautions, and compliance with applicable clinical safety standards. Coordinate vaccine and medication management, including storage, temperature monitoring, inventory control, administration processes, documentation, and response to storage or handling concerns. Maintain clinical emergency readiness, including emergency equipment, medications, staff BLS and emergency-response competencies, and participation in university emergency-preparedness activities. Coordinate clinical staff orientation, ongoing training, competency validation, and maintenance of required licenses and certifications. Lead and participate in quality-improvement activities, including incident review, chart audits, patient feedback, workflow evaluation, outcome monitoring, and development of corrective actions. Support compliance with applicable healthcare, privacy, safety, laboratory, and accreditation requirements and assist with regulatory and accreditation readiness. Collaborate with the director, medical director, campus partners, and local public-health authorities regarding communicable-disease surveillance, prevention, and response. Monitor clinical supplies, equipment, medications, and related expenditures; review invoices; coordinate purchasing; and provide input regarding budget and resource needs. Monitor clinical access, utilization, patient experience, workflow, and quality indicators and use findings to recommend operational improvements. Foster an inclusive, respectful, and collaborative clinical environment that supports student well-being, health equity, teamwork, and continuous learning. Participate in departmental and university planning and represent clinical operations on assigned committees or workgroups. Perform other duties as required or assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Bachelor of Science in Nursing from an accredited institution. Current, unrestricted license as a registered nurse in the State of Michigan. Current Basic Life Support certification. Three (3) years of relevant professional nursing experience in an ambulatory, primary care, urgent care, community health, public health, or comparable clinical setting. At least one (1) year of experience providing clinical leadership, staff supervision, team coordination, or comparable leadership responsibilities. Demonstrated knowledge of nursing assessment, clinical triage, medication administration, patient education, and appropriate escalation of care. Demonstrated ability to supervise staff, delegate responsibilities within applicable scopes of practice, assess competencies, provide feedback, and address performance concerns. Knowledge of infection prevention, immunization practices, medication management, clinical safety, and emergency-response procedures. Experience implementing clinical policies, standing orders, protocols, workflows, or evidence-based practices. Experience participating in quality improvement, incident review, chart auditing, performance measurement, or related patient-safety activities. Knowledge of applicable healthcare privacy, documentation, workplace safety, and regulatory requirements. Proficiency with electronic health records and the ability to use clinical and operational data to monitor performance and improve services. Demonstrated organizational, problem-solving, and decision-making skills, including the ability to establish priorities and respond effectively in a dynamic clinical environment. Demonstrated ability to communicate and collaborate effectively with healthcare providers, staff, students, university partners, public-health agencies, and community providers. Experience providing culturally responsive care and working effectively with diverse populations, including individuals who have experienced barriers to healthcare access. Ability to maintain confidentiality, exercise sound professional judgment, and work independently within established authority and scope of practice. Working Conditions:  Clinical office environment. Some travel may be required. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. Incomplete applications will not be considered. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact  harmonka@gvsu.edu . If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at  hro@gvsu.edu .  Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.    Application Deadline:   For full consideration, please apply by October 23. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Overnight-Safety-Specialist---Housing-and-Residence-Life--Adjunct-_R105114": {
      "title": "Overnight Safety Specialist - Housing and Residence Life (Adjunct)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPosition Summary: This role focuses on the evening/overnight security operations within residential buildings while fostering a supportive and responsible community. The Overnight Safety Specialist oversees the nightly building access and security program for GVSU residence halls by supervising and scheduling a team of Overnight Safety Student Assistants who monitor entrances, enforce access protocols, and assist residents and guests. Primarily working overnight and on a rotating schedule that includes weekdays and weekends, the specialist ensures proper staffing, responds to safety and behavioral concerns, and coordinates with Housing & Residence Life staff, campus security and police, and other university partners. Salary & Benefits:   Minimum starting salary of $44,000 commensurate with experience. Limited benefits package including medical, life insurance, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties:   Provide professional oversight of nighttime access and security operations across residential buildings. Conduct regular rounds among residential locations to provide visible professional support, assess operations, and assist Overnight Safety Student Assistants. Ensure student staff consistently follow established procedures for residents, guests, and individuals who do not have authorized building access. Respond to situations that exceed the role or authority of Overnight Safety Student Assistants and determine when concerns should be referred to Housing & Residence Life on-call staff, campus police/security, emergency services, or other university resources. Recruit, select, train, supervise, schedule, and evaluate a team of paid Overnight Safety Student Assistants. Provide regular supervision, coaching, feedback, recognition, and accountability for student employees. Create and maintain weekly and semester staffing schedules and ensure adequate coverage during established overnight safety operating hours. This includes managing call-offs, vacancies, substitutions, and unexpected staffing shortages. Serve as the professional point of contact for Overnight Safety Student Assistants when situations require consultation, additional support, or escalation. Assess safety concerns and determine appropriate next steps within established university protocols. Ensure appropriate documentation of significant access issues, guest concerns, policy violations, unusual circumstances, and safety incidents. Coordinate with Housing & Residence Life on-call team, or campus police, when circumstances or resident issues require continued follow-up. Develop and maintain written overnight safety operating procedures, expectations, training materials, shift protocols, and escalation procedures. Collaborate with Housing & Residence Life leadership to assess appropriate deployment of Overnight Safety staff based on building population, entrance configuration, incident history, and identified risk. Develop effective working relationships with all Housing & Residence Life staff including student staff and Resident Assistants, campus police, and other university partners. Participate in Housing & Residence Life safety planning, emergency preparedness, and operational meetings as appropriate. Perform other duties as assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Bachelor's degree, or equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Experience supervising, mentoring, coordinating, or providing leadership to employees, student employees, volunteers, or comparable teams. Demonstrated experience responding to challenging interpersonal, behavioral, safety, or customer-service situations. Ability and willingness to maintain a regular evening/overnight work schedule. Commitment to GVSU’s culture of Educational Equity. Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Ability to supervise, train, motivate, and hold student employees accountable. Strong organizational skills and demonstrated ability to manage complex employee scheduling. Ability to maintain appropriate professional boundaries and confidentiality. Strong interpersonal communication and conflict-management skills including critical thinking, deescalation, and crisis response. Ability to analyze operational information and identify patterns or areas requiring intervention. Ability to navigate online systems and software, and proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite (Excel, Word, PowerPoint) and other relevant technical software. Ability to work independently during evening and overnight hours while maintaining effective communication with departmental staff and leadership. Ability to establish collaborative relationships with Housing & Residence Life staff, campus police/security, and university partners. Ability to work effectively with students and community members from a wide variety of backgrounds and experiences. How to Apply: Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Adam Tate at tateam@gvsu.edu. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.  Application Deadline: Application review begins October 20th. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-General-Counsel_R105103": {
      "title": "Assistant General Counsel",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nThis position serves as a generalist within the Office of General Counsel and provides legal advice and representation to university leadership, administrators, and other campus partners across a broad range of legal matters and related issues. Salary & Benefits: Minimum starting salary of $120,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. This role requires a confident, client-facing attorney with demonstrated experience providing practical legal advice, exercising sound judgment, and communicating clearly with non-lawyer audiences. This position requires comfort handling varied and evolving issues, identifying legal risks, and recommending solutions aligned with the university’s mission and legal and compliance requirements. PRIMARY DUTIES MAY INCLUDE: 1. Provide timely, practical legal advice to university clients on a wide range of legal issues and support implementation of such guidance, as appropriate. 2. Manage and resolve legal questions from both university departments and generated by the Office of the General Counsel, including by identifying issues, asking questions, researching applicable law, assessing risk, delivering appropriate guidance, and drafting and reviewing written materials and communications. 3. Draft, edit, and review legal correspondence, responses to government agencies, opinions, contracts, agreements, background documents, litigation-related materials, and other legal or related documents as needed. 4. Represent or oversee the representation of the university in litigation, investigations, and administrative hearings, as needed, including preparing university employees for such processes and coordinating with outside counsel, and support or conduct internal investigations. 5. Provide direction and training to university departments on compliance, legal risk management, and institutional decision-making, including through conducting internal legal audits, developing and implementing systems and processes, and providing recommendations as necessary to ensure compliance with applicable requirements. 6 Define, support, and take actions needed to comply with the law and advise on a range of issues with high legal sensitivity/risk 7. Monitor and prepare comments, analysis, or proposed revisions on proposed, new, or existing federal and state legislation and regulation, as needed. 8. Recommend changes to university policies and practices to comply with new or updated legal requirements, manage risk, or achieve university goals. 9. Provide interpretations and analysis of existing university policies, and draft and coordinate potential policy revisions or new policies across different groups of university stakeholders to ensure legal compliance, consistency, and operational alignment. 10. Represent the Office of General Counsel in internal and external meetings, including on university initiatives and committees, and in meetings with external stakeholders. 11. Collaborate with other attorneys in the Office of General Counsel, ensuring consistent and legally sound guidance in alignment with university policy and mission and efficient delivery of legal services. 12. Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Juris Doctor from an accredited law school. Active license and good standing with the State Bar of Michigan or member in good standing of another Bar Association and willingness to seek admission to Michigan Bar Association. Minimum of eight (8) years of progressively responsible legal experience, with substantial experience in the active practice of law. Demonstrated experience confidently providing legal advice directly to clients. Strong legal research, analytical, writing, risk evaluation, and oral presentation skills. Ability to communicate complex legal concepts clearly and concisely to non-lawyers. Demonstrated professional judgment, discretion, and ability to manage sensitive matters and protect confidential information. Strong interpersonal skills, including an ability to develop and maintain professional and collegial relationships with students, colleagues, administrators, vendors, government officials, outside counsel, etc. Commitment to respectful engagement and professional integrity in a public university environment. PREFERRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Experience advising public sector, higher education, or similarly complex organizations. Exposure to a broad range of practice areas consistent with a generalist role, such as: employment and labor matters; civil rights or discrimination issues; student affairs and conduct matters; contracts and transactional review; litigation management; regulatory compliance, policy development and compliance; investigations and risk management; First Amendment or expressive activity; records and privacy matters; real estate law; immigration; and intellectual property. Experience balancing competing stakeholder interests in a mission-driven environment. Experience in managing legal operations and creating processes to efficiently deliver legal services. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and build productive relationships across diverse constituencies. Strong issue-spotting ability across varied legal topics. Ability to think critically and ask clarifying questions when facts are incomplete. Organized and able to manage multiple priorities effectively. Professional presence and interpersonal effectiveness in client-facing settings. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume, these documents are required. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Sara Pokorski (pokorsks@gvsu.edu).  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.    Application Deadline:   Application review begins October 19, 2026 and this posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Health-Campus/Clinical-Initiatives-Specialist---Health-Compliance_R105098": {
      "title": "Clinical Initiatives Specialist - Health Compliance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nThe GVSU Health Compliance Office is seeking a full-time staff member to collaborate with health programs supporting student clinical placements by identifying placement opportunities, facilitating required onboarding documents, managing the recognition program for preceptors, maintaining the placement database and creating various placement reports as requested. Salary & Benefits: Minimum starting salary of $61,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications and Education:   Registered Nurse with Bachelor’s degree in nursing or other health related degree Three to five years progressively responsible positions in clinical, health care administration or higher education health program setting(s). High level time management and organizational skills to manage multiple projects at once. Self-motivated with demonstrated ability to work both independently and as part of a team. Proficient in Microsoft Office including Outlook, Word, and Excel Proficiency in English language and grammar Excellent written and verbal communication skills Experience working with contract management.   Preferred Qualifications and Education:  Master’s degree in nursing, health administration or other health related degree Five to ten years progressively responsible positions in clinical, healthcare administration or higher education health program setting(s). Experience in occupational medicine or quality improvement. Experience working with a variety of databases and software systems. Experience in marketing or public relations.   Responsibilities – Essential Functions:  The Clinical Initiatives Specialist will: Apply knowledge of academic programs and clinical roles to interface with a variety of health professionals regarding clinical placements. Assist academic programs to identify and secure clinical placement sites for students. Manage the database for clinical placement and preceptor information to ensure timely and accurate data collection and entry. Create clinical placement and preceptor reports for administration each semester, and as requested. Manage the GVSU Preceptor Perks Program including coordination of preceptor enrollment, collaboration with other university departments, and creation of documents. Coordinate fit testing for academic programs and manage staff competencies. Assist with health compliance audits and student communication. Assist with laboratory, immunization, and other health compliance document processing. Assist with affiliation agreement contract coordination. Assist with department communication including website maintenance. Promote professionalism, confidentiality, cooperation and good working relationships with faculty, staff, students, and clinical practice sites.  Performs other duties as required/assigned by manger. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Rebekah Sabo (sabor@gvsu.edu).  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .   Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.   Application Deadline:   Application review begins Monday October 19, 2026 and this posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor-of-Soil-Science-and-Natural-Resources-Management---Department-of-Biology_R105121": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Soil Science and Natural Resources Management – Department of Biology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Department of Biology at Grand Valley State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Soil Science and Natural Resources Management to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in Soil Science, Natural Resources Management, or a closely related field. We seek a dynamic and innovative educator who specializes in soil science and management. The successful candidate will contribute to our curriculum by teaching entry and upper division courses and mentoring student research. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences.   Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. in Soil Science, Natural Resources Management, or closely related field. Current ABD candidates will be considered for a two-year contract with the provision that the Ph.D. must be completed by December 2028. Potential for successful college-level teaching and active research in soil science and management. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Evidence of effective teaching in soil science, soil management, and related natural resources courses at the undergraduate level, with a commitment to active and inclusive pedagogy. A record of applied scholarship that includes an active, student-centered research program. Experience with incorporating active learning strategies and innovative teaching practices inside and outside of the classroom. Demonstrated ability to collaborate across disciplines and contribute to the broader mission of the Department and University. Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate courses in soil science, soil management, and other related natural resource management courses based on departmental needs and candidate expertise. Develop and maintain an active research program that engages undergraduate and/or graduate students. Participate in curriculum development, student advising, and departmental service activities. Mentor the GVSU Soil Judging Club. Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts, with a nine-credit hour teaching load per semester. They are also expected to engage in scholarly activity, service, and a significant focus, which is equivalent to three credit hours of additional responsibilities in teaching, scholarly activity, and/or service depending on the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Salary & Benefits: Minimum starting salary: $80,300, commensurate with experience.  Comprehensive benefits package , including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.   Department: The Department of Biology at Grand Valley State University https://www.gvsu.edu/biology/ places an emphasis on teaching excellence using inclusive pedagogies, active scholarship with diverse learners, and equitable service to the university and community. Its 37 full-time faculty uphold the values of a liberal and sciences education while providing intensive engagement in the biological sciences. High-impact field and lab experiences and community collaboration are hallmarks of our relevant, interdisciplinary curriculum. There are numerous collaborative opportunities for faculty and students across a wide group of partners within the Department, across campus, and in the community.  College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest and most intellectually diverse college at Grand Valley State University, offering over 50 bachelor’s degree programs, badges, and certificates spanning the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered learning community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids, which includes the Robert C. Pew and Health campuses; and several regional centers. The University attracts undergraduate and graduate students with its high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovation. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following items to the online application: A cover letter addressing qualifications and your motivation to teach at GVSU Curriculum vitae Teaching philosophy statement Research statement including your motivation to conduct student-centered research Academic transcripts (unofficial transcripts issued to students are acceptable) The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically in the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Questions about the position may be directed to Dr. Todd Aschenbach (aschenbt@gvsu.edu). Application Deadline: Consideration of applicants will begin on November 6, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Parking-Services---TIBA-Support-Specialist--Temporary-Hourly-_R105076": {
      "title": "Parking Services - TIBA Support Specialist (Temporary Hourly)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nThis position is a part-time temporary position, not to exceed 29 hours per week. This position will assist the Parking Operations and Systems Technician Team with responding to parking lot phone calls, troubleshooting common entry and exit issues, and answering parking-related questions. This person will also support the ongoing operation of parking equipment by monitoring supplies and restocking machines with paper and other necessary materials. In addition, this position provides administrative support for parking events, including processing event parking requests and assisting with event setup and support with student employee supervision. Weekends and nights are required. Job Responsibilities: ·         Provide friendly and professional assistance when answering and responding to parking lot phone calls from customers experiencing issues. ·         Escalate complex equipment, access, or customer service issues to the appropriate staff member. ·         Monitor parking lot security cameras for gate and equipment damage. ·         Check and maintain parking equipment with paper and other supplies to maintain daily operations. ·         Responsible for processing daily and overnight visitor parking requests. ·         Assist with processing event requests and preparing validation codes as needed. ·         Assist with event parking as needed. ·         Working nights and weekends is required (Friday and Saturday, 6 p.m.- 12 a.m.) ·         Provide additional duties as needed/assigned. Driver's license will probably be required for truck use if need be. Compensation : $18.00 per hour. This position is not eligible for benefits.     How to Apply : Attach your resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Health-Campus/Winter-2027-Adjunct-Instructor---Kirkhof-College-of-Nursing_R103984-1": {
      "title": "Winter 2027 Adjunct Instructor - Kirkhof College of Nursing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nThe Adjunct Instructor provides clinical instruction and supervises undergraduate clinical experiences in a specific area of expertise (such as medical-surgical nursing, pediatrics, obstetrics, psychiatric, or community health nursing), a skills lab or a simulation environment. Primary Duties: Clinical teaching, supervision, and evaluation of students in a clinical setting, skills lab, or in simulation. Integrating KCON philosophy and organizing framework into clinical teaching. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: A Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN) degree. An unencumbered license as a Registered Nurse in the state of Michigan (or be eligible for licensure in Michigan as a Registered Nurse. Minimum of 3 years of experience as a Registered Nurse. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Commitment to working with diverse student and community populations. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Master’s degree (or higher) in Nursing. If MSN earned through an ADN-to-MSN or second degree to MSN degree program, BSN requirement is waived. Previous teaching and/or preceptor experience. Working Conditions: May involve a physically demanding, high-stress environment. May include frequent interruptions. Exposure to blood and body fluids, communicable diseases, chemicals, radiation, latex, and foul odors. Position requires reaching, bending, stooping, and handling objects with hands and/or fingers, talking and/or hearing, and seeing. How to Apply: Attach your cover letter, resume, documentation for any nationally recognized board certifications that you hold, and 3 references to the online application. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Barbara Hooper ( hooperba@gvsu.edu ).  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu or 616-331-2215. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.   Application Deadline:   Consideration of applicants will begin immediately, and the posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Health-Campus/Assistant-Technology-Specialist-Manager_R105041": {
      "title": "Assistant Technology Specialist Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nThe Assistant Technology Specialist Manager supports the maintenance of digital audiovisual systems for recording simulated sessions, including operation of various multimedia components used in the Simulation Center. JOB CLASSIFICATION & BENEFITS Minimum starting salary of $51,390; commensurate with experience Comprehensive benefits package includes medical, vision, dental, and life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources, and tuition waiver, effective on the date of hire. PRIMARY DUTIES 1. Assists in providing multimedia technology support for faculty, staff, and students specific to simulation events. 2. Assist with troubleshooting hardware and software issues and simulation equipment failures. 3. Assist the Multimedia Manager in setting up, resetting, storing, and providing technical support for simulation systems. 4. Prepares and maintains equipment, media, computers, and materials related to simulation learning. 5. Supports digital media use, web stream creation, content distribution, data backup, and troubleshooting. 6. Records and edits videos for faculty. 7. Works with the Multimedia Manager to conduct workshops and training sessions for faculty and staff on simulation multimedia technology and the video capture system. 8. Monitors and maintains inventory of multimedia supplies and equipment by keeping inventory management system up to date. 9. Ensures data confidentiality and security of recorded simulation content. 10. Support the development and implementation of policies, procedures, and best practices consistent with the Simulation Center mission, vision, values and strategic plan.  12. Actively seek professional development opportunities. 13. Supports the Simulation team during simulated educational scenarios when needed including patient simulator upkeep, programming, and operation. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Associate’s degree. Outstanding communication, organizational, attention to detail, and interpersonal skills and a team player. Experience with Information Technology (IT) systems. Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite with capacity to learn new software systems required. Demonstrated experience to successfully handle multiple demands efficiently. Demonstrated experience to successfully navigate challenging situations in the workplace, while using good judgement. Demonstrated experience to successfully maintain satisfactory work performance and attendance records. Ability to successfully maintain positive intercultural and interpersonal relationships. Flexibility in schedule is required due to Simulation Center event times. PREFERRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Bachelor’s degree. Database and multimedia maintenance and administration experience preferred. Two years of experience in simulation or technical services. Experience in higher education and simulation methodology preferred. Prior employment in a simulation center or healthcare education environment preferred.  WORKING CONDITIONS Standard hours: 7:00 AM – 4:00 PM; time flex based on events. Flex hours: 11:00 PM – 8:00 PM on days with evening events (approximately 1-2 days per week) If there is a Saturday event, a day off will be flexed during the following week, when feasible.   Normal office environment.  Some travel may be required. The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed and are not intended to be an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties and skills which may be required. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume, applications missing required documents will not be considered . The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Christine Adams (adamsc3@gvsu.edu).  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.    Application Deadline:   Application review begins Tuesday October 20, 2026 and this posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor---College-of-Computing_R105083": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - College of Computing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nThe College of Computing at Grand Valley State University is expanding and inviting applications for one tenured or tenure-track position across all ranks to join in Winter 2027 or Fall 2027. While all areas of Computer Science or closely related areas are encouraged to apply, preference will be given to candidates with a teaching and/or research background in Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity, Software Engineering, Data Science, Quantum Computing, and Human-Centered Computing/Design to fill the position.. We offer competitive salaries and generous start-up packages to support faculty in initiating and sustaining their research projects. Successful candidates will teach undergraduate and graduate courses, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, establish a robust, externally funded research program, and publish in peer-reviewed journals and conference proceedings. The faculty can also hire PhD students via our new PhD program in Computing and join various research institutes hosted in the College of Computing. Position Information • One tenured or tenure-track open rank position • Competitive salary and benefits • Generous start-up packages • Professional development • Industry collaboration opportunities • Interdisciplinary research opportunities with other colleges Applicants of all ranks require an earned doctorate in Computer Science, or a closely related field by the appointment date. Applicants for tenured Associate and Full Professor must have academic experience demonstrating excellent teaching and a strong scholarship record, with a history of successful grant writing. You can contribute by teaching in comprehensive degree programs and designing new programs to prepare career-ready talent in cutting-edge areas of computing. You will have the opportunity to expand our research and teaching portfolio and increase the impact via multidisciplinary collaborations. The College of Computing offers ABET accredited undergraduate programs, several minors, badges and graduate degrees. Grand Valley has over 24,000 students. The College of Computing has over 50 full-time faculty, approximately 1,000 undergraduate students, and 400 graduate students. We focus on experiential learning with opportunities for faculty-industry collaboration via the college's Applied Computing Institute, our Institute for Cybersecurity Education and Research, as well as the Artificial Intelligence Research and Innovation Institute, the West Michigan Trustworthy Artificial Intelligence Consortium, and newly established GVSU Hub on Advanced Computing Research. Salary & Benefits: Assistant Professor: Minimum starting salary of $105,000, commensurate with experience Associate Professor: Minimum starting salary of $125,000, commensurate with experience Full Professor: Minimum starting salary of $160,000, commensurate with experience Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses Mentor undergraduate and graduate students Establish a robust, externally funded research program Publish in peer-reviewed journals and conference proceedings Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. in Computer Science, or a closely-related field Record of teaching excellence commensurate with desired rank Record of research and scholarly publications commensurate with desired rank Commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion Record of external funding commensurate with desired rank Record of leadership and mentorship commensurate with desired rank Applicants of all ranks require an earned doctorate in Computer Science, or a closely related field by the appointment date. Applicants for tenured Associate and Full Professor must have academic experience demonstrating excellent teaching and a strong scholarship record, with a history of successful grant writing. Working Conditions: Normal office environment. Some travel may be required. How to Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following to the online application: (1) Cover Letter (2) Curriculum Vitae (3) Unofficial Ph.D. and/or terminal degree transcripts from awarding institutions (4) Teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching excellence, if applicable, including teaching evaluations for all courses taught in the last two years with department/school comparisons (and any other evidence of teaching excellence) (5) Research statement and sample of completed or in-progress research work If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact hodgsoal@gvsu.edu .  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .  Application Deadline: Application review will begin October 19 and will continue until the positions are filled. The Grand Valley campuses are in Allendale and downtown Grand Rapids, which is the second largest metropolitan area in Michigan. The area offers numerous cultural and recreational opportunities, a moderate cost of living, and a high quality of life.  Visit www.experiencegr.com or www.hellowestmichigan.com for information on living in the west Michigan area. Refer to our web site (www.gvsu.edu/computing) for additional information about our faculty, students, and university (www.gvsu.edu). To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Occupational-Safety-and-Health---Integrated-Engineering_R105107": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Occupational Safety and Health - Integrated Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nThe Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) program at Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is seeking a tenure-track faculty member to join the program at the Assistant Professor level. The successful candidate will primarily serve undergraduate students pursuing degrees and certificates in occupational safety and health. Primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses in occupational safety and health, both in person and through online modalities; supervising and mentoring students in applied learning experiences; conducting meaningful research, scholarship, and/or professional engagement that contributes to the advancement of the OSH profession and is disseminated to diverse audiences. For this position, expertise is especially desired in areas related to occupational health and safety management systems such as hazard recognition, assessment, and control; occupational health principles; industrial hygiene; safety training and program development; risk management; safety and environmental regulatory compliance; and the prevention of workplace injuries and illnesses across a variety of industries. Candidates should demonstrate knowledge of the historical and theoretical foundations of occupational safety and health, emerging trends and challenges affecting worker safety and well-being, and best practices that promote inclusion and responsive workplace environments. The successful candidate will be expected to foster partnerships with industry, professional organizations, and community stakeholders to support student success and advance the mission of the program. Salary & Benefits : Minimum starting salary $90,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.  Primary Duties: The successful candidate will teach multiple different Occupational Safety and Health courses offered each semester based on program needs and the individual’s strengths. Maintain clear and professional communication with students and colleagues. Use evidence-based teaching practices that emphasize active learning, inquiry, anti-bias education, and critical reflection, to support students as they learn to work effectively with their future colleagues. Engage in service activities at the program, unit, college, university, and community level. Conduct and disseminate meaningful scholarship Required Qualifications and Education: An earned terminal degree (PhD, EdD or other) or ABD (with expected graduation by August 2027) by an accredited institution in a related field to occupational safety and health. The ability to consistently teach face to face courses on GVSU City and Valley campuses. The ability to engage with a variety of technologies to deliver instruction face to face and in online synchronous, asynchronous, and hybrid modalities. Ability to deliver high-quality and engaging instruction to undergraduate students. Strong interpersonal communication and collaboration skills. An interest in contributing professional expertise to the community and profession A research agenda, with the ability to disseminate findings to a scholarly audience Preferred Qualifications and Education: Professional certification (i.e. CSP, CIH) Demonstrated experience in the practice of industrial hygiene. More than five years experience working in the occupational safety and health field. Experience teaching adult learners at the university or college level. Professional membership and involvement in ASSP, AIHA, or other professional organizations in the field. Working Conditions: Normal office environment. Some travel may be required. How to Apply: Please include a (1) cover letter and (2) CV. The cover letter must address one’s areas of expertise, especially as it relates to one’s knowledge and use experiential learning within a safety and health curriculum; one’s ideas related to community and K-12 involvement and program growth and recruitment; and how one has worked with adult learners thus far and what has or has not been effective in those teaching pursuits.  The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically. On the application, you will be required to provide names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses for three professional references (to be used only in the final stage of consideration). Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. The open position is for an Assistant Professor, subject to funding approval. Applications missing required documents will not be considered . If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact search committee chair Courtney Aloul at  aloulc@gvsu.edu . If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Application Deadline: Consideration of applications will begin immediately; preference will be given to applications received by November 15, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Public-Safety-Officer_R105100": {
      "title": "Public Safety Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nJoin the Grand Valley State University Police Department – Now Hiring Police Officers! The Grand Valley State University Police Department is seeking motivated, service-oriented individuals who are currently MCOLES licensed or licensable at time of hire and committed to building positive relationships within a vibrant campus community. This is an exciting opportunity to continue or launch your law enforcement career in a professional, supportive, and community-focused environment. Why Join Us? - Starting pay at $32.19/hour with the ability to start at a higher rate based on experience - Lateral transfer friendly – bring your experience and continue your career - Outstanding education benefits: 100% tuition waiver for employees 50% tuition waiver for household members and dependents - Comprehensive benefits package including health, dental, vision, and retirement contributions -Excellent work-life balance in a community-oriented policing environment -Opportunities for professional growth and specialized assignments Start your next chapter with a department that invests in your career, your education, and your future. Salary & Benefits: Minimum starting salary of $32.19/hour. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications: Associates Degree and/or equivalent experience. Applicants must be currently MCOLES certified or licensable as a police officer as defined by MCOLES at time of hire. Valid State of Michigan driver’s license with an acceptable traffic record. No criminal record, which will question credibility or public trust, expected of an officer. Possess good moral character as determined by a favorable comprehensive background investigation. Physical, mental and emotional ability to execute duties. Available to work any shift. Knowledge of current laws, standards and police procedures. Ability to learn and operate all communication equipment. Ability to maintain a satisfactory work performance and attendance record. Responsibilities: This is a uniformed and armed police position charged with enforcing state/local laws and the rules of the university. Patrols the campus in a marked unit. Responds to emergency calls for service. Arrest and issue citations as necessary. Provide backup assistance when dispatched. Provide first aid, scene control and/or coordination between backup units, ambulance and hospitals. Respond to and investigate complaints as received or as department requires. Testify at court and subsequent hearings. Serve subpoenas and warrants as requested. Revise and update all complaint reports and perform further actions as appropriate. Keep supervisor informed of progress. Provide surveillance of campus environment and report any unusual and/or unsafe conditions. Responsible for the safety and protection of the campus community, its personnel and property. Provide parking and crowd control at campus events. Act as a liaison between campus community and area law enforcement agencies. Responsible for care and maintenance of all equipment issued by the department. Willingness to exhibit proper attitude and demeanor to establish and maintain good public relations. Attend and complete by demonstrating proficiency all required in-service training programs which includes Medical First Responder training and certification . Performs other similar and related duties as directed by supervisor. How to Apply: Please submit an application with your cover letter and resume. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. You may add additional required documents during the application process. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Captain Kourosh Khatir at 616-331-3255 or khatirk@gvsu.edu. If you need technical assistance, please call Human Resources at 616-331-2215. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.   Application Deadline: Application closes Thursday, October 22, 2026. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Special-Education---College-of-Education-and-Community-Innovation_R105082": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Special Education - College of Education and Community Innovation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nThe Special Education program at Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is looking for two (2) tenure track faculty members to join the department of Teaching and Learning in the College of Education and Community Innovation at the assistant professor level. Both positions begin in August 2027 and require onsite, in-person responsibilities. Applicants must have earned a Doctorate, or ABD with expected completion by August 2027, in Special Education or a closely related field. Additionally, applicants must have: (a) demonstrated ability to teach courses related to students with disabilities; (b) a record of successful teaching at the K-12 level with students with disabilities; (c) demonstrated potential for teaching at the university level; and (d) a record of, or potential for, scholarship. Salary & Benefits: $66,000 - $68,000, commensurate with experience . Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources, and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the area of special education with emphasis on inclusive practices, academic content strategies, and specially designed instruction Supervise teacher candidates completing clinical experiences in K-12 classrooms Advise students in undergraduate and graduate special education programs Assist with the development, implementation, and continuous improvement of undergraduate and graduate courses and curricula Develop a research agenda and record of publications and presentations related to the discipline Provide service to the department, college, university, profession, and/or community Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership Required Qualifications and Education: Doctorate in Special Education or a closely related field (ABD, with expected completion by August 2027, considered) Minimum of 3 years of successful teaching in K-12 classroom setting Valid teaching certification/credentials (current or lapsed) Expertise in the areas of inclusive practices, academic content strategies, and/or specially designed instruction Demonstrated potential for teaching at the university level Record of relevant scholarship or evidence of an emerging research agenda in special education, disability studies, or related field The ability to disseminate findings to a scholarly audience Willingness and ability to teach courses in person, online, or hybrid format Willingness and ability to travel for instruction and student supervision Preferred Qualifications and Education: Specialized knowledge and experience working with students with specific learning disabilities (SLD), dyslexia and other language-based learning disabilities, high-incidence disabilities, or mild/moderate support needs Familiarity with high-leverage practices for students with disabilities, curricular access, structured literacy approaches, and/or Michigan's new science of reading requirements for K-5 instruction Record of quality teaching at undergraduate and/or graduate levels Evidence of student-centered and relationship-centered teaching Demonstrated commitment to utilizing and providing instruction on inclusive practices in college teaching Ability to coordinate and manage clinical-based K-12 experiences Experience with program leadership Experience with using various modalities of instruction, including face-to-face, online synchronous, asynchronous, hybrid, and other distance learning technologies Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues to support continuous program improvement, including curriculum development, program evaluation, and alignment of coursework and clinical experiences Working Conditions: Typical office environment. Some travel may be required. How to Apply: Apply online at jobs.gvsu.edu and select “Apply now.” Complete applications must include: Cover letter addressing one’s areas of expertise, especially as it relates to the required and preferred qualifications posted above Curriculum vitae Names and contact information of three professional references Graduate transcripts (unofficial acceptable for application; official transcripts required prior to on-campus interview) The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically. On the application, you will be required to provide names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses for three professional references (to be used only in the final stage of consideration). The initial round of interviews will be conducted virtually; second round interviews will be in-person on the Grand Rapids City Campus. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact search chair Julia Snider at snidejul@gvsu.edu . If you need technical assistance, contact Human Resources at hro@gvus.edu . Application Deadline: Consideration of applications will begin immediately with preference will be given to applications received by November 30, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Assistant-Professor---Educational-Leadership-and-Counseling--College-of-Education-and-Community-Innovation_R105079": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Educational Leadership and Counseling, College of Education and Community Innovation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nThe Educational Leadership and Counseling Department in the College of Education and Community Innovation at Grand Valley State University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of School Counseling beginning August 2027. Qualifications include a Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Counselor Education, or closely related field. ABD candidates will be considered if completed by August 2027, and the candidate has leadership experience. Primary responsibilities include the following: teach graduate courses in the School Counseling program, pursue scholarly interests, and provide service to the university, profession, and community. Salary & Benefits: $64,000-$66,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties: Teach three graduate course equivalents in the Fall and Winter semesters in the School Counseling program Willingness to teach in multiple instructional modalities, including face-to-face on the Grand Rapids campus, online synchronous/asynchronous, and hybrid formats Supervise graduate students in School Counseling practica and/or internships Pursue scholarly activities such as research, publication, writing and professional presentations Provide service to the institution (GVSU), profession, and community Advise graduate students Collaborate with colleagues for curriculum planning and preparation for accreditation Travel to school counseling program internship sites when necessary Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Counselor Education and Supervision, Counselor Education, or closely related field Current endorsement as a School Counselor or a current School Counselor license. Successful candidates will need to obtain Michigan School Counselor credentials within 1 year of their hire date (if not previously held) Demonstrated potential for university teaching Demonstrated interest in pursuing a scholarly agenda Demonstrated effective communication and interpersonal skills   Preferred Qualifications and Education: Successful university teaching experience at the graduate level (especially in school counseling) Evidence of accomplishment or strong potential of success in research, publication, grant writing, or scholarly writing Comprehensive understanding of the American School Counselor Association (ASCA) National Model Minimum of two years school counseling experience in a K-12 setting School counseling supervision experience Evidence of leadership and advocacy for the profession of school counseling Evidence of skills in curriculum development, assessment of college/university student learning, higher education accreditation, and clinical and school-based counseling practice Working Conditions: Typical office environment, some travel may be required How to Apply: Apply online at jobs.gvsu.edu and select \"Apply now. Completed applications must include: Cover letter addressing qualifications Curriculum vitae Names and contact information of three professional references Graduate transcripts (unofficial acceptable for application; official transcripts required prior to on-campus interview)   Applications missing required documents will not be considered. If you have questions about the position posting, please contact Dr. Karyn Rabourn, Search Committee Chair, at rabournk@gvsu.edu . If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at HRO@gvsu.edu .   Application Deadline: Review of completed applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Presidential-Operations---Initiatives-Specialist_R105077": {
      "title": "Presidential Operations & Initiatives Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nThis position provides flexible, hands-on support across administrative, operational, project-based, and engagement-focused work, responding to evolving priorities and peak workload periods. This role works closely with the Director of Presidential Operations and other members of the President’s Office, as well as partners across the university. Salary & Benefits: Minimum starting salary of $50,000, commensurate with experience. Limited benefits package including medical, life insurance, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. PRIMARY DUTIES: 1. Presidential Operations & Administrative Coordination Oversee a variety of operational priorities and logistics in collaboration with the Director of Presidential Operations, including budgeting and finance, Board of Trustees, and commencement support Prepare and provide quarterly budget updates using Workday. Assist with preparation and coordination for Senior Leadership Team (SLT) and other cross-discipline teams organized by the President’s Office, including agendas, materials, logistics, and follow-up. 2. Project Management & Strategic Initiatives Oversee project management and coordination for university initiatives in collaboration with the Director of Presidential Operations, Support the implementation of the university’s strategic framework, Reach Higher Together. Develop and maintain systems for tracking timelines, deliverables, and outcomes related to university priorities. Analyze project requirements, identify gaps and dependencies, and proactively and independently advance assigned projects. 3. Web & Communications Management Manage and coordinate routine updates for the President’s Office and Board of Trustees websites, through the applicable content management system (CMS). Evaluate website content for accuracy, timeliness, consistency, and alignment with institutional priorities. 4. Office Coverage & Executive Coordination Provide backup support to the President’s Office Executive Assistant and Executive Assistant to the President during periods of high need or staff absences. Ensure continuity of office operations during peak periods. Provide additional support for the President’s Office events and engagement team as needed. Provide support for Board of Trustees-related activities as needed. 5. Hospitality, Facilities & Front-Facing Support Serve as a front-facing resource supporting the needs of faculty, staff, students, and guests within the President’s Office (Valley Campus) and John Russell Building (City Campus). May be required to provide hospitality and help create a welcoming, professional atmosphere at the John Russell Building on the City Campus. 6. Other Duties Identify and implement opportunities to continuously improve President’s Office systems, workflows, documentation, and processes to strengthen efficiency, coordination, and operational effectiveness. Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Demonstrated experience providing administrative, operational, project, or program support in a professional setting. Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities, deadlines, and competing demands. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across teams and divisions. High level of professionalism, discretion, judgment, and attention to detail. Proficiency with standard office software and collaboration tools; ability to learn institutional systems such as Workday and CMS platforms. PREFERRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Experience working at Grand Valley State University, in higher education, executive offices, or similarly complex organizational environments. Familiarity with Workday and budgeting or financial tracking processes, including quarterly reporting. Experience supporting senior leadership, boards, councils, or executive-level meetings. Experience with project management related to strategic initiatives. CMS or website management experience. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume, these documents are required. Applications missing required documents will not be considered . The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Cori Kahler ( kahlerco@gvsu.edu) .  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu .    Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.    Application Deadline:   Application review begins Monday October 5 and this posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Health-Campus/Regional-Director-of-Philanthropy-Southeast-Michigan--Adjunct----Institutional-Advancement_R105069": {
      "title": "Regional Director of Philanthropy Southeast Michigan (Adjunct) - Institutional Advancement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nPosition Summary:   This position is responsible for cultivating and expanding relationships with alumni, parents, friends, and supporters of Grand Valley State University (GVSU) throughout Southeast Michigan. This position plays an important role in building GVSU's presence and philanthropic pipeline in the region by identifying new prospects, strengthening relationships with existing supporters, and creating meaningful opportunities for individuals to engage with and invest in the university. Working as part of the Philanthropy Team and in partnership with academic and university leaders, this position develops and implements strategies to identify, qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward individual donors. This role has significant responsibility for managing a portfolio of prospective and current donors and for developing new relationships. Salary & Benefits:   Minimum starting salary of $95,000 commensurate with experience . Limited benefits package including medical, life insurance, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.  Primary Duties:   Develop and implement an annual plan for the Southeast Michigan region to identify, qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward prospective and current donors in support of short- and long-term university fundraising goals. Serve as a primary relationship manager for a portfolio of prospects and donors, including individuals with the capacity to make gifts of $15,000 or more annually. Develop individualized strategies to deepen relationships, identify philanthropic interests, and advance prospects through the fundraising cycle. Build and expand GVSU's alumni and philanthropic network throughout Southeast Michigan by identifying new prospects, engaging alumni and supporters, and developing relationships with community leaders. Partner with university and academic leaders, faculty, volunteers, and colleagues across Institutional Advancement, including the areas of corporate giving and foundation relations, to develop compelling opportunities for philanthropic investment that align donor interests with university priorities. Manage and advance major gift opportunities, including developing strategies, coordinating internal resources, preparing proposals and solicitation materials, and supporting successful gift requests. Lead or coordinate projects related to complex philanthropic gifts, working collaboratively with colleagues in Philanthropy, Donor Relations, Gift Planning, Corporate Giving and Foundation Relations. Prepare and execute major gift materials, including briefing materials, proposals, stewardship communications, and other donor-facing materials. Collaborate with Donor Relations to develop appropriate stewardship strategies for donors and prospects. Track and report relationship management activity using the advancement CRM, including identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship activity. Provide regular updates regarding portfolio activity, opportunities, and progress toward fundraising goals. Maintain accurate and timely records of donor and prospect interactions, including contact reports, key conversations, strategies, and next steps. Maintain an active portfolio of prospects and donors with capacity for significant philanthropic support and consistently move relationships forward through meaningful engagement. Represent Grand Valley State University and the Grand Valley University Foundation at university events, alumni activities, community gatherings, volunteer opportunities, and professional organizations throughout Southeast Michigan. Perform other duties as required or assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Bachelor's degree or knowledge equivalent to that which normally would be acquired by completing a four-year college degree program in an appropriate field (e.g., communications, public relations, marketing, business, nonprofit management, or related field). Five to seven (5-7) years of fundraising, advancement, relationship management, sales, business development, community engagement, or related experience. Demonstrated experience to independently identify, qualify, and build a portfolio of new donor relationships rather than relying on an established donor base. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain meaningful relationships with individuals from diverse backgrounds and levels of professional experience. Ability to identify areas of shared interest between an individual's passions and the priorities and mission of the university. Understanding of, or demonstrated ability to learn, the principles of successful fundraising, including prospect identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship. Strong interpersonal and human relations skills, with the ability to effectively interact with university leadership, faculty, alumni, donors, volunteers, and community members. Ability to work independently, take initiative, manage competing priorities, and follow through on commitments. Curiosity and a desire to learn about donors, the university, and the communities in which GVSU operates. Ability to communicate in a clear, compelling, and persuasive manner that builds confidence and trust. Ability to develop and manage projects from inception through completion. Computer proficiency, including working knowledge of CRM databases and standard office technology. Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Master’s degree. Successful higher education fundraising experience in raising major gifts. Prior experience working with senior-level executives and professionals. Strategic mindset: ability to be forward-thinking and to create long-term relationships for maximum gifts, rather than focusing on single transactions. Ability to adapt within formal, confidential, and various business and social settings. Proven successful experience working in a complex environment on multiple and diverse projects. Working Conditions:   Travel throughout Southeast Michigan and work evenings and weekends as needed. How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Institutional Advancement at advancement@gvsu.edu. If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.  Application Deadline:   Application closes Monday October 19, 2026. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Grand-Rapids-MI-Pew-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Early-Childhood-Education---College-of-Education-and-Community-Innovation_R105042": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Early Childhood Education - College of Education and Community Innovation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nThe Early Childhood Education program at Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is looking for a tenure track faculty member to join the Department of Teaching and Learning in the College of Education & Community Innovation at the assistant professor level to serve primarily undergraduate students taking courses in early childhood education that also fulfill general education requirements. Primary responsibilities include: teaching undergraduate courses in early childhood and early childhood special education, both in person and using online modalities; supervising candidates in the field; participating in service obligations; and conducting meaningful research that will be disseminated to a diverse audience. For this particular position, expertise is especially needed as it relates to the philosophies, theories and historical foundations of early childhood education; the impacts on child development & how childhood is influenced by the current context; knowledge in the seminal texts of the field and contemporary insights; and movements that help lead to increased equity, culturally responsive and sustained teaching, and inclusive learning environments. Opportunities may also exist for teaching courses for graduate students and courses connected to observation and documentation, learning through play, child guidance, and collaborating with families. Salary Range & Benefits:   $64,000 - $66,000, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties:   Teach undergraduate courses in early childhood, both in person and using online synchronous and asynchronous modalities Supervise candidates in diverse settings and using online tools Maintain clear and professional communication with students and colleagues Use evidence-based teaching practices that emphasize active learning, inquiry, anti-bias education, and critical reflection, to support students as they learn to work effectively with young children and their families and future colleagues Engage in service activities at the program, unit, college, university, and community level Conduct and disseminate meaningful scholarship Required Qualifications and Education:   An earned PhD or EdD degree or ABD (with expected graduation by August 2027) by an accredited institution in Early Childhood Education, Early Childhood Special Education, Child Development, Instruction and Curriculum, or Teacher Education with an emphasis in Early Childhood, Family Studies, or Human or Child Development At least three years experience working with a range of young children and/or young children with disabilities and their families in an education setting (ages infant/toddler, preschool, and/or early elementary) The ability to consistently teach face to face courses on GVSU City and Valley campuses An interest in teaching coursework that addresses the philosophies, theories, and historical foundations of early childhood education The capacity to travel to provide clinical supervision in diverse settings The ability to engage with a variety of technologies to deliver instruction face to face and in online synchronous, asynchronous, and hybrid modalities Demonstrated excellence in delivering high quality and engaging instruction to adult learners A passion for teaching culturally relevant, responsive, and sustaining pedagogies and preparing educators to work with diverse populations A commitment to educating the whole child and promoting inclusive teaching practices Strong interpersonal communication and collaboration skills A research agenda, with the ability to disseminate findings to a scholarly audience An interest in working as part of a team in developing, implementing, and evaluating programs and activities that support programs in early childhood education An interest in contributing professional expertise to the community and profession Preferred Qualifications and Education:   Experience teaching adult learners at the university or college level, particularly as it relates to the Foundations and Philosophies of Early Childhood, Observation and Documentation, Child Guidance, Collaborating with Families, and Play-Based Curriculum courses Knowledge and familiarity with the seminal texts of the field, including those that relate to the history of education and more contemporary insights around the application of these principles More than five years experience working with diverse populations as a licensed educator within an early childhood setting, particularly in early elementary settings The ZA, ZS, or ZN teaching endorsement, as awarded by the Michigan Department of Education Experience or familiarity with teaching courses that meet General Education requirements and serve primarily first and second year students from a variety of academic disciplines Professional membership or involvement with NAEYC or CEC Experience teaching with the Blackboard Ultra Learning Management System A promising track record of disseminating research in peer-reviewed journals, books, and through scholarly presentations Experience with program evaluation and/or with accreditation standards Working Conditions:   Typical office environment, some travel may be required. How To Apply: Apply online at jobs.gvsu.edu and select \"Apply now\". Please include a (1) cover letter and (2) CV. The cover letter must address one’s areas of expertise, especially as it relates to one’s knowledge and use of theoretical frameworks to explain and enact early childhood pedagogies; one’s experience working with young children and their families; and how one has worked with adult learners thus far and what has or has not been effective in those teaching pursuits. The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically. On the application, you will be required to provide names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses for three professional references (to be used only in the final stage of consideration). Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. Applications missing required documents will not be considered. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact search committee chair Dr. Kathryn Ohle at ohlek@gvsu.edu .  If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Application Deadline: Consideration of applications will begin immediately and the posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University; preference will be given to applications received by October 25th, 2026. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor-of-Earth-Surface-Materials-and-Systems---Department-of-Geology_R105055-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Earth Surface Materials and Systems - Department of Geology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Department of Geology at Grand Valley State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Earth Surface Materials and Systems to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in Geoscience or a closely related field. We are an exclusively undergraduate department that values a strong commitment to quality and inclusive teaching. We welcome adaptable candidates who have the capacity to lead a geology curriculum broadly in Earth Surface Materials, Geomorphology, and Hydrogeology. Candidates should also demonstrate a commitment to an active research program within those disciplines with the promise of successfully obtaining extramural support. The ideal candidate will have research interests that can directly integrate undergraduate students, with preference given to those with the potential to develop research within the Great Lakes region (e.g., surface water dynamics, glacial geomorphology, coastal processes, watershed hydrology, or groundwater). We also seek candidates focused on innovative modeling, field-based, or applied approaches and an interest in building notable external partnerships and/or interdisciplinary collaborations. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. Salary/Benefits:  Minimum starting salary: $76,500, commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package , including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver, effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. in the Geosciences or a closely related field. Potential for successful college-level teaching and active research in an undergraduate department. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Active research program broadly within the disciplines of Earth Surface Materials, Geomorphology, and/or Hydrogeology. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Proven teaching experience at the college/university level.  History of extramural research support.   Responsibilities: Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts, with a nine-credit hour teaching load per semester. They are also expected to engage in scholarly activity, service, and a significant focus, which is equivalent to three credit hours of additional responsibilities in teaching, scholarly activity, and/or service depending on the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Department: Faculty in the Department of Geology are scholar-teachers and leaders in disciplines as diverse as 1) geochemistry and hydrogeology; 2) geoscience education; 3) sedimentology, paleoclimate and climate science; and 4) geophysics, geodynamics and tectonic processes. We place an emphasis on teaching excellence using inclusive pedagogies, active scholarship with diverse learners, and equitable service to the university and community. The Department of Geology includes nine tenure-track faculty members, two affiliate faculty members, two visiting faculty members and approximately 65 students in our majors (Geology, Geology-Chemistry, Climate Science). We also serve several hundred Integrated Science and Pedagogical Content Knowledge for Elementary Teaching majors with a focus on science education, as well as a new and rapidly growing Environmental Science program. The department values high impact teaching, undergraduate research experiences, and collegial faculty-student interactions (see www.gvsu.edu/geology).   College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest and most intellectually diverse college at Grand Valley State University, offering over 50 bachelor’s degree programs, badges, and certificates spanning the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered learning community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids, which includes the Robert C. Pew and Health campuses; and several regional centers. The University attracts undergraduate and graduate students with its high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovation. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following items to the online application: A single document including: a cover letter addressing qualifications and your motivation to teach at GVSU, curriculum vitae, and the contact information of three references Teaching philosophy statement Research statement (This is a summary of research activities, including those involving undergraduate students, and possible facilities/equipment needs.) Graduate transcripts (Unofficial transcripts issued to students are acceptable.) Sample of completed or in-progress research activity The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically to the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Questions about the position may be directed to search committee chair Associate Professor Ian Winkelstern (winkelsi@gvsu.edu). Application Deadline: Consideration of applicants will begin on November 9, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor-of-Violin-and-Viola---Music--Theatre--and-Dance_R105056": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Violin and Viola - Music, Theatre, and Dance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Department of Music, Theatre, and Dance at Grand Valley State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Violin and Viola position to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a graduate degree in violin and/or viola performance. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. Salary/Benefits: $69,397; commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications and Education: High level of artistry on violin and viola. Graduate degree in violin and/or viola performance. Demonstrated potential to recruit violin and viola students. Potential for successful college-level teaching and ongoing creative activity. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Completed DMA in violin and/or viola performance. Demonstrated success in recruiting and teaching a collegiate violin and/or viola studio. Responsibilities: Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts, with a nine-credit hour teaching load per semester. They are also expected to engage in scholarly/creative activity, service, and a significant focus, which is equivalent to three credit hours of additional responsibilities in teaching, scholarly/creative activity, and/or service depending on the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Primary duties will include the teaching and mentoring of university level violin and viola students with additional teaching in the core music curriculum as assigned. The teaching assignment will be dependent upon the successful candidate’s qualifications and interests, and the needs of the department.   Department: The Department of Music, Theatre, and Dance provides an intensive performing arts education within a liberal education. Our six-degree programs are offered by 34 full-time faculty with over 150 performances by faculty, guest artists, and students each year. The department is located on the Valley Campus in the Haas Center for the Performing Arts. College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest and most intellectually diverse college at Grand Valley State University, offering over 50 bachelor’s degree programs, badges, and certificates spanning the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered learning community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids, which includes the Robert C. Pew and Health campuses; and several regional centers. The University attracts undergraduate and graduate students with its high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovation. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following items to the online application: A cover letter addressing required and preferred qualifications and your motivation to teach at GVSU. Curriculum vitae which includes contact information for three professional references. Teaching philosophy that includes examples of the candidate’s past successful student recruitment activities. Graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts issued to students are acceptable). A one-page document with YouTube links to a 20-minute performance video and a 20-minute private violin and/or viola lesson or masterclass, both recorded within the past three years. The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically to the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Questions about the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Joel Schut (schutjo@gvsu.edu). Application Deadline: Consideration of applicants will begin on October 15, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter.  To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor-of-Microbiology_R105054": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Microbiology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Department of Biomedical Sciences at Grand Valley State University (GVSU) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Microbiology to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in Microbiology or a closely related field. A successful applicant will be expected to: teach introductory microbiology courses and associated labs; develop an active research program at GVSU which engages undergraduate and graduate students; and be a contributing member of the broader community both inside and beyond the institution. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. Salary/Benefits: $80,300; commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package , including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. in Microbiology or closely related field. Current ABD candidates will be considered for a two-year contract with provision that the Ph.D. must be completed by December 2028. Evidence of training that has prepared the candidate to teach college microbiology classes. The ability to develop an active research program at GVSU that engages students. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Experience teaching advanced microbiology classes. Post-doctoral research experience.   Responsibilities: Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts, with a nine-contact hour teaching load per semester (usually three classes). They are also expected to engage in scholarly activity, service, and a significant focus, which is equivalent to three contact hours of additional responsibilities in teaching, scholarly activity, and/or service depending on the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Department: The Department of Biomedical Sciences at Grand Valley State University places an emphasis on teaching excellence using inclusive pedagogies, active scholarship with diverse learners, and equitable service to the university and community. Its 36 full-time faculty uphold the values of a liberal education while providing intensive engagement in the biomedical sciences. High-impact lab experiences in classes and research labs are a hallmark of our relevant, interdisciplinary curriculum. There are numerous collaborative opportunities for faculty and students across a wide group of partners within the department, across campus, and in the community. For more information about the research in the department and the resources available, visit http://www.gvsu.edu/bms . College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest college and intellectually diverse at Grand Valley, offering over 50 bachelor’s degrees and a growing number of advanced degrees in the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids, which includes the Robert C. Pew and Health campuses; and several regional centers. The University attracts undergraduate and graduate students with its high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovation. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following documents to the online application: A cover letter addressing your qualifications and motivation to teach at GVSU. A curriculum vitae with the contact information of three references. A statement of teaching philosophy. A research plan that includes how you will involve students in your research. Graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts issued to students are acceptable). The online application portal will allow you to attach these five documents electronically to the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to an on-campus interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Questions about the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Dr. Doug Graham (grahamdo@gvsu.edu). Application Review: Consideration of applicants will begin on October 19, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Admissions-Recruiter_R104919": {
      "title": "Admissions Recruiter",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nThis position will represent GVSU to prospective students, parents, secondary school counselors and other individuals or organizations involved in the college selection process. This position will manage assigned recruitment territory through prospect relationship management, leveraging partnerships, relationships, and admissions and enrollment data to maintain consistent recruitment activity, to increase prospective student interaction, and influence admission yield. Salary & Benefits:  $51,000 - $55,000, commensurate with experience Comprehensive benefits package including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources, and tuition waiver effective on date of hire. Primary Duties: Represent GVSU both on and off-campus to prospective students, parents, secondary school counselors, and other individuals or organizations involved in the college selection process. Advise prospective applicants on the admissions process, academic programs, support resources, and opportunities at the university through in-person and virtual appointments, small group presentations, phone calls, and emails. Manage assigned recruitment territory through prospect relationship management, leveraging partnerships, relationships, and admissions and enrollment data to maintain consistent recruitment activity, to increase prospective student interaction, and influence admission yield. Review materials submitted by applicants for the purposes of making admission decisions. Develop and implement recruitment and yield projects as assigned. Participate in information sessions, open houses, and other recruitment and yield events. Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Bachelor’s degree. Minimum of two (2) years of experience in customer service, recruitment, education, sales, or related field. Strong cross-cultural skills. Strong presentation, interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Commitment to GVSU’s vision of inclusion and equity.  Valid driver’s license and ability to travel to remote locations and to load and transport admissions materials, displays and other equipment. Ability to work evening and weekend hours required throughout the year, especially during the fall travel season. Preferred Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Higher education experience. Experience with customer relationship management systems (CRM). College/university admissions experience or experience working with middle school or high school audiences. Bilingual in Spanish . Working Conditions: Monday through Friday, with evening and weekend work required throughout the year. Admissions recruitment occurs primarily by in-person appointments, but also by small group presentations, zoom appointments, phone calls and emails. Extensive travel is required. Department/Division: Admissions Office, Enrollment Development and College Futures Campus: Allendale, MI How to Apply: A cover letter and resume are required. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. Apply online at GVSU Careers . If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Faye Spears at spearsf@gvsu.edu . If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future. Application Deadline:  Review of applications will begin immediately, and the posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the university. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Assistant-Professor---Robert-B-Annis-Water-Resources-Institute_R105015": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Robert B. Annis Water Resources Institute",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nSummary: The Robert B. Annis Water Resources Institute (AWRI) at Grand Valley State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Water Resources to begin Fall 2027. Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in ecology & evolution, freshwater science, aquatic chemistry & toxicology, limnology, ecological engineering, or a closely related field. We welcome applications from researchers working at any spatial scale, in any freshwater system (lakes, rivers, streams, wetlands, or groundwater), at any level of biological or environmental organization, and using any mode of inference, from empirical to computational. Applicants who can augment our existing strengths in conservation and restoration ecology, environmental sensing and detection, human and environment interactions, and aquatic biogeochemistry and contaminants are particularly encouraged to apply. The mission of AWRI is to integrate research, education, and outreach to enhance and preserve freshwater resources. Over our 40-year history we have built a culture of interdisciplinary collaboration, deep community engagement, and a commitment to faculty and staff well-being. Individuals from all backgrounds, including from underrepresented groups, are encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must be able to effectively engage with students, colleagues, and community members with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences. Required Qualifications and Education: Ph.D. focused in ecology and evolution, freshwater science, aquatic chemistry and toxicology, limnology, ecological engineering, or closely related field. A demonstrated record of publishing quality research in their field of expertise. Potential for successful college-level teaching and mentoring of undergraduate and graduate students in research. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, such as the ability to interact with others with respect and cultural humility. Preferred Qualifications and Education: Success obtaining extramural funding for research. A desire to work in a collaborative, interdisciplinary environment. Interest in engaging in a robust community engagement and education program. Responsibilities:  Tenure-track faculty in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences are typically hired on nine-month contracts. Tenure-track faculty at the Annis Water Resources Institute (AWRI) will balance their teaching and research workloads in collaboration with the AWRI Executive Director. A minimum of three-credit hours of teaching is required per year and remaining workload is allocated to research with specific expectations defined using credit-hour equivalents. Tenure-track faculty are also expected to engage in service within and beyond the institution, as well as community engagement activities that suit the successful candidate's interests and the department’s needs. Salary & Benefits:  Minimum starting salary: $80,000; commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package , including medical, vision, dental, life insurance, generous employer retirement contributions, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.   Department: AWRI is a world-class research facility situated on Muskegon Lake in Muskegon, Michigan, on the shores of Lake Michigan. The institute houses a dozen faculty, numerous research staff, and a robust M.S. program in Biology with an aquatic science emphasis. Located 30 miles from the main Grand Valley State University campus, AWRI maintains state-of-the-art analytical and molecular laboratories, a mesocosm facility, and two research vessels. Please visit the AWRI website ( www.gvsu.edu/wri ) for more information on our faculty, facilities, research programs, educational programs, and outreach initiatives. College: The College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) is the largest and most intellectually diverse college at Grand Valley State University, offering over 50 bachelor’s degree programs, badges, and certificates spanning the natural and mathematical sciences, the fine arts and humanities, and the social sciences. CLAS is a student-centered learning community that welcomes individuals from various backgrounds and engages in critical inquiry, extending knowledge to enrich and enliven individual and public life. Our faculty are active teacher-scholars committed to offering students an accessible and community-engaged liberal arts and sciences education that prepares them for meaningful careers. There are many opportunities for collaboration within CLAS and across a broad group of partners on and off campus. We are interested in attracting a cohort of new faculty committed to these core values as we seek to foster a community where members from all backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive together. University: Grand Valley State University (GVSU) is a public comprehensive institution located in West Michigan. GVSU has multiple campuses—the Valley Campus in Allendale, Michigan; the City Campus located in downtown Grand Rapids; and several regional centers, including AWRI in Muskegon, Michigan. The University attracts 22,000 undergraduate and graduate students with high-quality programs and state-of-the-art facilities and has been nationally recognized for innovativeness. The greater Grand Rapids metropolitan area has a population of more than one million people, is affordable, and offers rich cultural amenities. GVSU is located within easy driving distance of Chicago, Detroit, Lake Michigan beaches, and many other superb summer and winter recreational venues. Information on Community Resources can be found on the Community Resources page of the GVSU Human Resources website . How To Apply: Apply online at GVSU Careers . To be considered for the position, applicants must upload the following items to the online application: A cover letter addressing qualifications and your motivation to join AWRI Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement The names and contact information of three references   The online application will allow you to attach these documents electronically to the same file location. Applicants selected for interviews will be required to submit official transcripts prior to the interview. If you need assistance, email Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu . Questions about the position may be directed to Dr. Stuart Jones, Allen and Helen Hunting Executive Director of AWRI at jonesstu@gvsu.edu. Application Deadline: Consideration of applicants will begin on October 16, 2026. The posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University thereafter. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Muskegon-MI/Deckhand-on-the-WG-Jackson--Temp-Hourly-_R105017": {
      "title": "Deckhand on the W.G. Jackson (Temp Hourly)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nJob Summary : The deckhand's primary role is to maintain safety and security onboard the WG Jackson during cruises. They inspect and operate several pieces of equipment throughout the day and and follow direction of the captain to ensure safe operation of the vessel. Compensation : $19.00 per hour. This position is not eligible for benefits.   Essential Functions Represent Grand Valley State University and the Annis Water Resources Institute in a professional matter Maintain proper vessel security I.A.W. Ref (a) Carry out duties of the Deckhand as outlined in the PMS program I.A.W. Ref (b) Report vessel deficiencies to the on duty Captain and correct same as required Take the lead in all emergency evolutions including, man overboard, fire fighting, taking on water etc. Maintain the sanitation, overall condition, appearance, and safety of the vessel Master the proper operation of all weight handling equipment Monitor the safe and proper operation of all vessel weight handling and scientific equipment Complete routine inspections and maintenance as directed by the Fleet Captain Become familiar with the vessel’s radio, navigation equipment, and controls in order to call for assistance and safely return the vessel to port in case of an emergency Master the required safety drills Work at the direction of the Captain to ensure the safe operation of the vessel Handle lines as required Monitor aft deck safety Learn the proper operation and maintenance of scientific equipment used on the aft deck, including bathythermographs, water samplers, dredges, corers, plankton nets, trawl nets, and CTD’s Be knowledgeable about and respond to questions from passengers regarding safety, educational programs, and equipment Maintain aft deck discipline when no science instructor is present Ensure all ship’s equipment is secured properly and ready for sea Learn, follow, and promote proper safety procedures I.A.W. Ref (c) Assist science instructors and researchers as required, in coordination with the on duty Captain Act as a host and guide for occasional dockside tours and demonstrations Other duties as assigned by the Vessel Captain and Fleet Captain Required Qualifications: Ability to swim and comfort working on and around open water Ability to stand for extended periods, climb ladders, and work on a moving deck in wet, cold, or hot conditions Ability to follow verbal and written instructions from the Captain quickly and accurately, especially during drills and emergencies Willingness to take charge of emergency evolutions (man overboard, fire, flooding) after training and stay calm under pressure Strong attention to detail for inspections, equipment checks, and securing gear before departure Ability to work as part of a small crew and communicate clearly with the Captain, instructors, and fellow crew Comfort interacting with the public, including K-12 students, teachers, and community groups, and answering basic questions about safety and equipment Willingness to perform cleaning, painting, and routine maintenance tasks Mechanical aptitude and willingness to learn the operation of scientific sampling equipment Ability to represent GVSU and AWRI professionally in appearance, language, and conduct How to Apply : Attach your resume. The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact Eric Hecox ( hecoxe@gvsu.edu ). If you need technical assistance, please contact Human Resources at hro@gvsu.edu. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
    },
    "https://gvsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Allendale-MI/Community-Director---Housing-and-Residence-Life--Adjunct-_R105013-1": {
      "title": "Community Director - Housing and Residence Life (Adjunct)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nThis position is a live on residential role primarily focused on the management of a campus housing unit consisting of 400-800 residents. This position will supervise a student staff of Resident Assistants (RA) for the community and create an inclusive community development plan focused on supporting all residents and will also be responsible for the daily administrative, operational, and facilities duties within the community assigned. Staff in this role will have direct contact with students through the restorative justice process, student CARE and outreach as well as through recruitment of RA Staff.   Salary & Benefits: Minimum starting salary of   $43,888.00 , commensurate with experience. Limited benefits package   including medical, life insurance, wellness resources and tuition waiver effective on date of hire.   Primary Duties:   Supervision and management of residential area housing consisting of 400- 8 00   residents with undergraduate and graduate staff teams of between five and fourteen .     Advising and academic support for residents through one-on-one interactions and community curriculum development.    Development and facilitation of community development plans to enhance   the residential   student experience and student retention. This includes application of foundational student development theoretical models and guiding literature, development of community learning goals through the facilitation of the Housing and Residence Life (HRL) curriculum, participation in the larger community engagement series, and specific connections with campus resources to aid students in successful navigation of the college experience.    Maintain assigned community budget to support programming and overall administration of the community. Prioritize student success and engagement through budgeting and planning with short- and long-term goals.   Respond to and report incidents in alignment with HRL protocol and compliance regulations.   Conduct and conflict resolution management   utilizing   Maxient , and restorative justice measures as part of the HRL and GVSU Conduct and Conflict Resolution protocol.   Audit and   maintain   community facilities through room condition reports, public area inventory, art inventory, administer charges for damages, complete health & safety inspections to create and   maintain   a safe and secure living environment .     Lead summer tours of incoming students through summer orientation onboarding.    Serve in an on-call duty and crisis response after   hours   response team. Respond to students in crisis, incidents, and emergency situations 24-7 while serving in this capacity.   Collaboration with campus partners and department/division committee participation.    Perform other duties as required/assigned by leadership. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   ​ Bachelor's degree .   Minimum   one   (1)   year experience working in a university residential setting .   Experience working with students in a college setting .     Experience working on a team.    Peer leadership experience.   Demonstrated critical thinking skills.    Interest in engaging and educating   students     Excellent   verbal,   written , interpersonal   communication   and organizational skills .   Commitment to   GVSU’s   vision of diversity, inclusion, and equity.      Preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities:   Master's   d egree in   s tudent   a ffairs or related field .   Experience managing and supervising teams.    Budget oversight experience .   Performance in restorative practices and/or student conduct management .   Student program development and implementation experience .   Experience supporting students academically .   Leadership and facilities management of an assigned living area .   Experience providing academic support to college students .   Strong technology/computer skills .   Working Conditions:   Normal office environment. Some travel may be   required .   Must be available for varied hours, extended workdays, weekends, and on-call emergencies as events and activities require.       How to Apply:   Attach your cover letter and resume.   The system will allow you to attach these documents electronically. If you have questions about the position or the posting, please contact  Brandie Tenney at tenneyb@gvsu.edu . If you need technical assistance, please   contact   Human Resources at   hro@gvsu.edu .     Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States and must not require sponsorship now or in the future.     Application Deadline:   Application review begins September 17, 2026. This posting may be closed at any time at the discretion of the University. To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  Must have the physical stamina to work long hours and/or more than 5 days per week.  The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The information contained in this job description is for compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act as amended by the ADA Amendment Act (2008), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and other applicable federal and state laws that prohibit discrimination on the basis of disability. GVSU will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. See   www.gvsu.edu/equalopportunity/ . TDD Callers: Call Michigan Relay Center at 711 (in State) or 1-800-833-7833 (out of State).   Grand Valley State University is an Antidiscrimination/Equal Opportunity Employer, which includes providing equal opportunity for protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. University policy extends protections to additional identities."
